-- ── X. THE REACHABILITY THEOREM ─────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- Neither session had this. It outranks everything above it in this file.

-- d-two-states-are-unreachable : verify.py:236-256 derives, and the package
--   ENFORCES, that two of the eight cannot occur in static verification.
--
--   The argument, quoted: the joint formula is the conjunction of the base
--   formulas; "Conjunction is monotone: adding clauses can only remove
--   models. Therefore any base group UNSAT ==> the joint formula is UNSAT,
--   which makes B=UNSAT together with J=SAT unreachable -- it would require
--   the conjunction to have a model that one of its own conjuncts lacks."
--
--   B=UNSAT with J=SAT is exactly 010 and 011 -- SHADOW_PARADOX and
--   MIRROR_PARADOX. The two states this whole line of work leans on.
--
--   The authoritative split is two frozensets, NOT the enum's visual
--   grouping, which is stale and disagrees with them:
--     STATIC_REACHABLE_VERDICTS     = {VERIFIED, CONTRADICTION, PARADOX,
--                                      TIMEOUT}
--     PROOF_EVOLUTION_ONLY_VERDICTS = {MIRROR_PARADOX, SHADOW_PARADOX,
--                                      BASE_FRAMES, METAPARADOX}
--
--   And it is checked, not merely declared: check_static_invariant raises
--   LatticeInvariantError, and it is called at two live sites
--   (verify.py:604, :707). This constraint CAN fail. It is being checked.

-- d-adr-0002-ran-our-own-standard : ADR 0002 "Static lattice reachability,
--   world-model ontology, and meta-rule authorization", Accepted 2026-08-09,
--   says of its predecessor: ADR 0001 "stated B/J/C reachability in prose but
--   did not derive it, DID NOT ENFORCE IT" and asks "Is every named state in
--   the eight-state lattice actually reachable? ... Nothing checked whether
--   the ordinary static semantics can produce them or whether the bits were
--   being mistaken for typed stage state."
--
--   That is this family's governing standard, stated independently, applied
--   to an eight-name lattice, and remediated with an enforced invariant --
--   in the repository slated for deletion, eight months before the P0 work
--   restated the same standard. It is the strongest single argument against
--   deleting it. See GC-PLT-6.

-- WHICH EIGHT THIS FILE GRADES. The peer asked, correctly, before the tower
-- multiplies. Answer: Tyler's eight and this package's eight are ASSUMED to
-- be the same eight, on the following evidence -- his statement arrived
-- directly into a discussion of this lattice; 8/16/32 is the Z2^n doubling
-- this lattice already has; and "convergence states" names its C bit. That
-- is strong but circumstantial. UNEXCLUDED alternative: he is enumerating
-- classical paradox TYPES (liar, Russell, sorites, ...), which would make the
-- numerical correspondence a coincidence. Recorded as an assumption so it can
-- be checked, not as a fact.


-- ── XI. WHAT THE THEOREM DOES TO THE RELIGION WORK ──────────────────────────

-- d-paradox-holding-is-not-conjunction : the central consequence.
--   Paradox-holding -- parts individually fail, the whole is held anyway --
--   IS the pattern B=UNSAT, J=SAT. Under conjunction that is not merely
--   unmodelled or hard to reach. It is IMPOSSIBLE, by a one-line monotonicity
--   argument the package already derives and enforces.
--
--   So the failure of a classical common-tenet intersection is a THEOREM, not
--   a modelling preference. Anyone who builds a gate whose admission
--   condition is "can this tradition reach a joint-SAT holding of these
--   tenets" has written a gate that, if the joint is the conjunction of the
--   tenets, NOTHING CAN EVER PASS. That is the anti-pattern in its other
--   direction: a constraint that cannot be satisfied is not a gate.

-- d-the-escape-is-named-in-source : and it is not a lattice extension.
--   The same comment says those states are reachable "only under partial or
--   proof-evolution semantics, WHERE BASE AND JOINT OBSERVATIONS COME FROM
--   DIFFERENT CANDIDATE FORMULAS and monotonicity therefore does not apply."
--
--   Read for the religion work: a person holding contradictory tenets is not
--   holding a satisfiable conjunction of those tenets. The whole they hold is
--   A DIFFERENT FORMULA from the conjunction of the parts they state. The
--   stated tenets and the held commitment are observations of two different
--   candidates. That is a substantive claim about what a religion IS under
--   this machinery, and it is forced, not chosen.

-- d-redirect-to-proof-evo : *** WRONG AS STATED -- SEE SECTION XVI. proof_evo
--   returns ProofStatus, never a Verdict, and contains zero of the four
--   excluded verdicts. The monotonicity result and everything drawn from it
--   stands; only "the machinery exists" fails. What stood here: *** the
--   machinery for doctrine formation is
--   logic/proof_evo.py -- evolve():912, evolve_proof():1105, tournament
--   selection with a UCB-1 bandit over mutation operators -- and NOT ppl.py
--   or cycle_detector.py. Both sessions were reading the wrong two files.

-- This QUALIFIES section III. "Classical intersection is projected onto the
-- b3=0 slice" is true and soft. The hard statement is that its target states
-- are unreachable under conjunction by theorem. Keep the hard one.


-- ── XII. THE FOURTH BIT MAY ALREADY BE LATENT IN THE SOURCE ─────────────────

-- d-lossy-means-the-semantics-parameter : "lossy B/J/C" is now readable.
--   The bit pattern does not record WHICH SEMANTICS produced the observation.
--     static semantics          B and J from ONE formula    -> 01x impossible
--     proof-evolution semantics B and J from DIFFERENT       -> 01x possible
--                               candidate formulas
--   Same three bits, two regimes, different reachable sets. The dropped
--   parameter is the regime. That is what the docstring is warning about,
--   and it answers GC-PLT-2: the encoding is lossy in the REGIME, not in the
--   verdict count.

-- d-b3-convergence : flagged as a convergence, NOT a proof.
--   The regime parameter is binary, it is exactly what makes the paradox
--   states reachable, and proof-evolution is sequential and candidate-
--   dependent -- the observation depends on when you measured and on what.
--   That is measurement-dependence, which is what d-b3-candidate proposed on
--   independent grounds. Two routes arriving at one binary coordinate is
--   evidence for it. It is NOT a derivation: "depends on which candidate" and
--   "depends on the order of measurement" are not yet shown to be the same
--   distinction, and showing it is now the concrete form of GC-PLT-3.


-- ── XIII. THERE ARE NO DYNAMICS ─────────────────────────────────────────────

-- d-cycle-detector-is-an-analyser : CycleDetector.classify(trace) takes a
--   Sequence[Verdict] that already exists. _find_convergence_strict scans it
--   for consecutive pairs. There is no state-advancing function anywhere in
--   logic/ except proof_evo's own evolve(). So the 4-cycle is a pattern
--   DETECTED IN histories, not a law that GENERATES them, and the
--   convergence:degeneration ratio is a statistic over a trace produced
--   elsewhere.
--
--   The peer reached this conclusion by a different route -- "composing
--   transitions is associative, so there is no operator for order to attach
--   to." Associativity is the wrong reason: function composition is
--   associative and non-commutative, so associativity alone never gives
--   order-independence. The right reason is that _TRANSITION_MAP is a
--   CLASSIFIER over observed (src, dst) pairs, not an action of operators on
--   states. There is nothing to compose. Same conclusion, better grounded.
--
--   For the religion work: you cannot run doctrine formation FORWARD in
--   cycle_detector. It can only score a history. Forward motion lives in
--   proof_evo -- a second, independent reason for d-redirect-to-proof-evo.


